\enlargethispage{2\baselineskip}
\subsection{Cross-Suite Generalization}
\label{sec:cross_suite}

The cliff zone exhibits a suite $\times$ method interaction: at $k \in [0.85, 0.95]$, Object tasks collapse under random removal while Spatial tasks degrade gracefully (Figure~\ref{fig:crosssuite}, Table~\ref{tab:pi05_crosssuite}). L2 norm maintains a near-baseline plateau on both suites ($\leq 2.4$\,pp at $k \geq 0.85$). CV$\,{=}\,$0.055 correctly predicts this plateau on both suites despite qualitatively different cliff profiles, confirming CV as a model-level diagnostic. Token importance heatmaps reveal a center-high, edge-low spatial pattern (Figure~\ref{fig:token_heatmap}, Appendix~\ref{app:token_heatmap}), formalized by a hypergeometric model $H(N{=}768,\, C{=}91,\, T{=}6)$: cliff onset at $k{=}0.96$ (10\% collapse probability) narrows to $k{=}0.90$ (90\%), RMSE${=}$0.025 (Appendix~\ref{app:cliff_model}). The model captures onset sharpness at high retention but does not generalize to moderate retention ($k \leq 0.7$), where compensatory mechanisms may emerge (\S\ref{sec:analysis}). The cross-suite difference is driven by Object's concentrated criticality ($C{=}91$) versus Spatial's distributed pattern ($C \approx 5$).

