\label{fig:entanglement_scoring}
\end{figure*}

SmolVLA's pixel-shuffle $4{\times}4$ folding concatenates 16 patches per token (Figure~\ref{fig:pixel_shuffle}), creating non-overlapping receptive fields that collapse SR to ${\leq}$6.6\% at $k{=}0.95$ across all suites and methods (verified on two checkpoints). No scoring function can identify dispensable tokens because all carry spatially entangled information.

\subsection{Position Disruption vs.\ Information Loss}
\label{sec:zeromask}

$\pi_{0.5}$'s random pruning at $k{=}0.9$ collapses to 9.0\% on Object despite removing only 10\% of tokens. We compare hard pruning (removes tokens, re-indexes positions) with zero-masking (zeros embeddings, preserves positions): zero-masking preserves 98.0\% SR while hard pruning collapses to 9.0\%. The 89\,pp gap is entirely attributable to position disruption---$\pi_{0.5}$'s linear connector tolerates complete signal destruction but not index shifts.

\subsection{Scoring Method Sensitivity and CV Diagnostic}
\label{sec:cv_sensitivity}

CV predicts where scoring method choice becomes critical (Figure~\ref{fig:method_gap}). $\pi_{0.5}$'s Object gap at $k{=}0.95$ reaches 24.2\,pp, exceeding X-VLA's maximum (1.4\,pp) by $17{\times}$. At $k{=}0.5$, $\Delta$(R$-$L2) on Object is $+$36.0\,pp ($\pi_{0.5}$), $+$27.2\,pp (X-VLA), confirming random preserves spatial diversity while L2 clusters correlated tokens.

\textbf{Architecture-dependent scoring.} The optimal scoring method depends on the connector (Table~\ref{tab:attention_pruning}). On X-VLA at $k{=}0.5$, attention recovers ${\geq}$92.8\% while L2 falls 8--27\,pp below random; on $\pi_{0.5}$, attention is catastrophically harmful on Object ($-$30.4\,pp). On Long, attention cliffs $-$26.5\,pp from $k{=}0.95$ to $k{=}0.85$, then plateaus (Figure~\ref{fig:cliff_highlight}).

\textbf{Disruption peak.} Random pruning on $\pi_{0.5}$ is \emph{more} destructive at intermediate than aggressive ratios. On Long at $k{=}0.9$, the margin peaks at 46.9\,pp (L2 80.4\% vs.\ Random 33.6\%; Figure~\ref{fig:cliff_highlight}); at $k{=}0.5$ all methods converge within ${\sim}$2\,pp (Appendix Figure~\ref{fig:margin_profile}). The V-shaped random recovery arises from position aliasing under scattered removal (Section~\ref{sec:analysis}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.75\linewidth]{figures/paper/fig_cliff_highlight.pdf}
\caption{Scoring method determines cliff behavior on $\pi_{0.5}$ Long (five-seed mean $\pm$ s.d.\ shaded; $n{=}5$). L2 degrades monotonically; attention exhibits a sharp cliff from $k{=}0.95$ to $k{=}0.85$ ($-$26.5\,pp), then plateaus; random exhibits a V-shape with collapse at $k{=}0.9$ (46.9\,pp gap vs.\ L2) and recovery at $k{\leq}0.7$.}
\label{fig:cliff_highlight}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Importance margin gradient.} L2's advantage shrinks with task complexity on $\pi_{0.5}$ at $k{=}0.85$: Object $+$93\,pp $>$ Goal $+$35.2\,pp $>$ Long $+$33.2\,pp, as multi-step tasks distribute critical information across heterogeneous norm profiles.

\subsection{VTR Recovery}
\label{sec:vtr_recovery}

VTR uses $M$ learned queries cross-attending over all 128 source tokens (${\sim}$22M parameters, ${\sim}$5\% of SmolVLA), recovering from 0.0\% to over 60\% SR at 75\% compression ($M{=}32$: 60.1 $\pm$ 1.3\%, 3 seeds; Table~\ref{tab:vtr_main}). A parameter-matched MLP adapter achieves 63.4 $\pm$ 2.6\% ($p{=}0.15$), confirming the gain stems from re-encoding, not cross-attention. Cross-suite: Spatial 53.2\%, Goal 56.4\%, both from 0.0\%.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{VTR re-encoding on SmolVLA (LIBERO-Object). Hard pruning collapses all configurations to 0\%; VTR recovers ${\geq}$57\% at up to 87.5\% compression.}
\label{tab:vtr_main}
\begin{tabular}{lccr}
\toprule
Configuration & $M$ & Compress.\ & SR (\%) \\
\midrule
\textit{Baseline (unpruned)} & 128 & 0\% & 44.8 \\
\textit{Hard pruning (any)} & -- & 5--50\% & 0.0 \\
\midrule
Cross-attn VTR & 32 & 75\% & 60.1 $\pm$ 1.3 \\
Cross-attn VTR & 16 & 87.5\% & 56.9 $\pm$ 1.3 \\
MLP adapter & 32 & 75\% & 63.4 $\pm$ 2.6 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\textbf{Efficiency.}\label{sec:efficiency} For $\pi_{0.5}$, $k{=}0.9$ cuts attention FLOPs by ${\sim}$16\% while preserving full SR; VTR at $M{=}16$ achieves 87.5\% compression with ${\leq}$1.1\,pp below passthrough.

